Z||000016||kempo||09-03-2001||10:40 PM||||The kid just got a fiero and the s2000 didn't know what he was doing.  There's no reason for this nonsense...  We all know that an s2000 could kill our stock fiero's.  A win is a win, whether it's a miss-shift, bad start, engine blows up, whatever...  So cut the guy a break.  If you pulled up next to a Ferrari and beat it in say a quarter because the guy miss-shifted, it's your win, and you get bragging rights.  <BR>I agree with firstfiero:  It was a stupid statement.||||reg||1
Z||000017||b lo 0||09-04-2001||12:02 AM||||There is a point to be made here.  I have driven many Vtec Hondas (an '89 CRX Si and 2 '99 Civic Si's and yes, I have driven an S2000).  You have to rev the crap out of them to get them to go.  Not so much the Civics, as the Vtec kicks in at about 4200 at 3/4+ throttle, but the S2000 kicks in at about 7000 rpm.  Monster.  You really gotta know what you're doing to drive an S2000 that fast.  And trust me, driving the S2000 that fast is FRIGHTENING.  You're like, la tee da, then 7k passes and you're rocketing along up to 9000 rpm and grabbing second.  Like driving a Civic Si, only more comparable to a ZR1 Corvette (driven one, too).  Drive a 2K Miata Sport really hard, and multiply that by 10.  You get the idea.  Sure, he had a faster car, but you had faster wits.  I'd be way more proud of that than my car any day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">       <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.vstoreauto.com.com" TARGET=_blank>Audio Imagery</A>, budget-priced car audio supplies from people like Monster Cable, Sony & more!||||reg||1
Z||000018||Turbo88||09-04-2001||12:17 AM||||Hmm, well let me offer some thoughts. I have eaten every S2000 I have raced in the LS1 T/A. My Formula will eat my T/A. I bought my T/A for 21k about a year ago which is about 10-15k less than the S2000. When I finish my Formula, I will rev damn near as high as the S2000, I will make more horsepower than my T/A and I will have less than 10k invested to do this. So if you are looking for bang for the buck, the Fiero has it. If you are looking for looks, buy a Vette and still eat an S2000. If you are looking for import performance, the integra can be made to run like hell. All a matter of choice but I believe the S2000 is an overpriced toy targeting people with no comprehension of automotive engineering. Yes they are neat, no they are not worth the money. But Honda/Acura did the same thing with that damn overpriced eurotrash wanna-be NSX. For 1/2 the money, you can run with it in a Z06 vette. And for a little more, you can buy a used Ferrari F355 and out do it in every contest imagineable. <P>Can't ya tell I have something against Honda Motors and their poor attempts at highly priced all performance automobiles?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Well I'll say that the Civic is actually a very good car!<P>...<p>[This message has been edited by Turbo88 (edited 09-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||FieroLisa||09-04-2001||12:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<BR><B>All a matter of choice but I believe the S2000 is an overpriced toy targeting people with no comprehension of automotive engineering</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Like you said, buying a car is alll a matter of personal choice. The S2000 is actually very affordable considering the other cars in its class, and the fact that it *is* a good performer is a plus too... <BR>I guess I just get frustrated cause everyone spouts off the information they read about some cars (not incl. those who have driven one of course) but isn't that where everyone else is getting the fiero fire myths? It seems to me that a lot of the opinions on this topic are opinions that people have heard from this or that... and that's the same way with the Fiero myths. And aren't you sick of hearing "heeeey didn't all those catch on fire or something? They're death traps!"<BR>Ah well I'm freakin tired and in a bad mood so I'm gonna quit typing before I pi$$ someone off  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||Formula88||09-04-2001||01:03 AM||||Ok, people, all personal opinions aside, if your Fiero can beat an S2000, post the time slips or shut up.  Everyone has a story about what they can beat.  If your Fiero is modded out the wazoo, then take on an S2000 modded out the wazoo and see how you do.  This isn't meant to offend or defend anyone in particular, but it's becoming the problem that opinions are like a$$holes.  Everyone's got one.  If you can prove what you're saying, do so.  If not, then you're no better than the people who trash Fieros without ever having ridden in one.<P>I have no problem believing a competent driver in a good running stock Fiero V6 can take an S2000 if the S2000 driver doesn't drive the car properly.  A VTEC does need to be driven differently than most cars to get the best performance out of it, and if you haven't learned the car yet, you won't get very impressive times out of it.  Now, if both drivers are well acquainted with their cars and drive them to the best of their ability, I don't believe the stock Fiero has a snowball's chance in Florida.||||reg||6
Z||000021||artherd||09-04-2001||02:31 AM||||Heh, thanks, corvette vs NSX in quality. I needed that.<BR>(do agree about the F355, there should be a better word than 'automobile' to describe what it is. Heaven on a Stick maybe?)<P>I promise I won't make a crack about camaro drivers' intelligence.  Oops.<P>By the way guys, 'v-teck' dosen't 'kick-in' either, and it's not nessicarily a top-end enhancer.  Rather it is two seperate cam lobes (intake only typically but both on newer motors/generations.) One cam lobe has low/no overlap for low-rpm torque, the other high overlap for high-rev torque.    <P>Despite this, the 2.0litre engine in the S2000 needs to be reved like you are pissed.  4,000rpm dumps won't exactly 'bog' the car to the point of near-stall, but you won't go very fast either.    Droping the clutch at 7500+ is usually best...<P>I personally have test-driven an S2000 (amazingly enough alone, they must have not only been short-handed, but INSANE, but hey.) and it is geared to take advantage of the revs.  But in the end it just dosen't feel right compared to cars with comprable or  even higher (race) redlines. <P>Still, a cool car, and YES it's a toy, that's the great part!!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<BR><B>But Honda/Acura did the same thing with that damn overpriced eurotrash wanna-be NSX. For 1/2 the money, you can run with it in a Z06 vette. <P>...Well I'll say that the Civic is actually a very good car!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000022||Joe Torma||09-04-2001||03:30 AM||||I have a magazine article on 2 S2000s.  One stock and one a Mugen(sp?) performance version.  They squeezed 15hp and like 10lbft of torque additional for an additional(I think) $20,000.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>Vtec is a very cool thing, but it provides NO torque in a <B>usable</B> range for street.  <P>Horsepower @ rpm<BR>(SAE net) 240 @ 8300 <BR>  <BR>Torque (lb.-ft. @ rpm) 153 @ 7500<P>What are you going to do with torque spinning up so fast near redline with that vtec working?  You'd need to really know that car well to "race" it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Formula88||09-04-2001||09:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>By the way guys, 'v-teck' dosen't 'kick-in' either, and it's not nessicarily a top-end enhancer.  Rather it is two seperate cam lobes (intake only typically but both on newer motors/generations.) One cam lobe has low/no overlap for low-rpm torque, the other high overlap for high-rev torque.    <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I suggest a recent article in Sport Compact magazine (last month, I believe) for those who'd like to learn exactly what VTEC is.  You are partly correct.  In a DOHC setup, there is a mild cam lobe over the valves, with an aggressive cam lobe in between with a rocker stub that doesn't contact anything.  The profiles are so different, you can visually see the center lobe has more life and duration.  At the changeover rpm, a pin locks the center rocker that's not touching anyting with the other rocker's that are on the valves.  That's how it switches from a mild cam profile to an agressvie one.  "Kicking in" may not be a technical term, but it accurately describes the point at which the VTEC switches cam profiles.  Also, I don't understand what you mean by not a top end enhancer.  The aggressive profile is designed for high rpm power - how could that NOT be a top end enhancer?<P>On some VTEC engines, only the intake cam has it.  On others, both intake and exhaust do.  On the new i-VTEC in the Acura RSX, it not only does the normal VTEC changeover, but the computer can advance and retard the camshaft at will, altering the torque curve to deliver the best power at any given rpm.  It also helps with emissions, and an EGR valve is not required since the same thing is accomplished with cam timing.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000024||Turbo88||09-04-2001||03:45 PM||||Well 6 of one 1/2 dozen of the other. As far as bang for the buck, the S2000 doesn't have it. Sorry, look at the numbers. You can drive them until your heart is content but the numbers tell the story. If you are looking at them from a bang for buck performance stand point. It can't touch a fiero or an FBody. Sorry. Why? because if you spend that much money modding a fiero vs what the S2000 costs, the fiero eats the S2000. None, stock for stock it can't. But so what? In the same price range is the C5 Vette, the SLP fbodies, the Cobra-R, the Subaru Impressa WRX (all wheel drive), and the Acura RSX. Most of the above totally out perform the S2000 or run with it and are cheaper. And if you want to take this a step further, for 11 grand, you can run low 10s to high 9s in a Yamaha YZF R1 or Honda CBR 929RR and eat everything listed so far. Just not in the rain!<P>Anyway it is a matter of opinion coming from someone that likes to go fast before anything else. I think opinions are more common than assholes because everyone seems to have more than one (opinion that is). <P>As for timeslips, if noone believes that I've got a mid 13 second car, I'll post it as soon as I get home. I didn't think that was so unrealistic. The Formula's engine is out right now because I am putting in a different cam/valvetrain for the Pontiac Uprising this October in Ark City, Kansas. <P>BTW, anyone wanting details on the Uprising, let me know, I will get them for you..  ||||reg||1
